  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">2024 Mozambique Drought and Food Insecurity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">2024 Mozambique Drought and Food Insecurity - Bilateral with the BRC-FL</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gaza State - Mozambique, implementation by BRC-FL in coordination with CVM and IFRC</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">More than 20 million people live in the areas currently affected by drought, with 2.7 million Mozambicans already facing food insecurity (IPC Phase 3+, crisis). It is feared that the situation will worsen for 1.25 million people in these areas in the coming months, possibly reaching IPC Phase 4 (severe food insecurity/emergency).</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mozambique is currently facing a food crisis due to prolonged drought. During the rainy season, less than half of the usual rainfall was recorded, and in the central and southern parts of Mozambique, it has been exceptionally dry and warm, largely as a result of El Ni&#xf1;o. These weather conditions have led to damage to agricultural crops, resulting in a below-average harvest. In Mozambique, 15% of the total planted area (690,000 hectares) in the southern and central regions has been destroyed. The poor harvests in the region are putting increased pressure on the already precarious food reserves of local communities and national governments.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The peak of needs in the drought-affected areas was expected from October to January, the traditional &quot;lean season&quot; at the end of the dry season. However, current forecasts show that needs will continue to rise in the first quarter of 2025, with possible negative effects of La Ni&#xf1;a in the central and southern parts of the country. Improvement in food security is not expected until after the harvests in April 2025. Additionally, CVM (Mozambique Red Cross) has already begun preparing the response, and fieldwork for the market assessment has been completed. Systems for the food voucher program are currently being set up, so the response can be quickly mobilized once funds are secured. BRC-FL will use a combination of emergency CASH assistance, nature-based programming and climate-smart agricultural approaches to adress these issues.</narrative>
  </description>
